WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.800
Many organizations federate cloud access through an identity provider.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.140
Instead of managing separate cloud passwords for every user,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.360
employees sign in through single sign-on using corporate identity controls.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.980
Groups, conditional access, MFA, device posture,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.880
and role assignment can then determine which cloud roles a person may assume.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.500
This centralizes lifecycle management:

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.080
when someone changes roles or leaves the organization,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.100
access can be adjusted from the identity provider.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.040
Multi-factor authentication is especially important for sensitive cloud access.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.500
A password alone should not be enough to reach powerful administrative roles.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.440
MFA does not prove that every action is safe,

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:46.300
but it reduces the chance that a stolen password becomes immediate control-plane access.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.720
Conditional access policies can add more context, such as trusted devices, location,

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.860
risk signals, or stronger authentication for higher-risk actions.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.480
Authentication and authorization are different.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.740
Authentication answers who the user or workload is.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.680
Authorization answers what that principal can do.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:10.020
A user may authenticate successfully through SSO and MFA but still be unauthorized to

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.600
delete a database,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.380
modify a firewall rule, or read a secret.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.920
Cloud IAM security depends on both:

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.420
strong sign-in controls and carefully designed permissions.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.280
Temporary access reduces standing privilege.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.660
Role assumption, just-in-time access, privileged access workflows,

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.280
and short-lived credentials can let authorized users perform tasks without keeping

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.740
powerful permissions active all the time.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.480
Long-lived access keys and standing administrator grants create operational risk because

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.060
they can be copied,

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.280
forgotten, or misused.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.300
Where long-lived credentials still exist, they should be owned, rotated, monitored,

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.120
and reduced over time.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.700
Just-in-time access is especially useful for powerful roles.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.040
Instead of leaving administrator access active all day,

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.340
an engineer can request time-limited elevation for a specific task.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.880
The approval, duration, reason, and resulting actions can be logged.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.420
This creates a better record for audit and incident response while reducing the window in

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.960
which a compromised session can cause damage.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.780
Long-lived keys should be treated as exceptions, not the default.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.680
If a key is needed, it should have an owner, purpose, scope, rotation plan,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.020
storage expectation, and monitoring.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.440
Many environments still contain old keys created for scripts, vendors, or migrations.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:38.020
Finding and reducing those keys is often one of the highest-value IAM cleanup projects.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.840
Federation also improves offboarding and emergency response.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.060
If a user leaves the company or a credential is suspected of compromise,

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.520
central identity controls can remove or restrict cloud access quickly.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:55.620
Local-only cloud users and unmanaged keys are harder to find and harder to disable.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.560
That is why mature programs reduce standalone accounts and monitor exceptions closely.
